"\section=1"
"\name=authors"
"\summary=generate git repository AUTHORS from identity register"

namespace authors
{
  {
    "<options> <identities> <dir>",

    "\h|SYNOPSIS|

     \c{\b{authors} [<options>] <identities> [<dir>]}

     \h|DESCRIPTION|

     The \cb{authors} command updates the \cb{AUTHORS} file of a git
     repository so that it lists every person who contributed to the
     repository once, by their preferred name, in alphabetical order. The
     contributors are the people the identities (name and email) of the
     repository history belong to, as recorded in the identity register
     <identities> (see ENTRIES below for details). The history identities are
     the commit authors and the \cb{Co-authored-by} trailer values.

     If <dir> is specified, then it should be a directory inside the working
     tree of the repository to update or, with \cb{--all}, the directory to
     search for repositories. Otherwise, the current working directory is
     assumed.

     \cb{AUTHORS} starts with a fixed preamble that says who the IW4x
     authors are, followed by one entry per person, sorted
     case-insensitively in the bytewise order so the order is the same on
     every machine. \cb{AUTHORS} only grows: the people it already lists stay
     listed, whether or not the history carries them.

     The update proceeds the same way as that of \l{mailmap(1)}:

     \ol|

     \li|Prepare every repository: generate its \cb{AUTHORS} and establish
     that it can be installed, committed, and, if requested, pushed. If any
     repository fails to prepare, then nothing is changed in any of them.|

     \li|Print the plan: for each repository whose \cb{AUTHORS} is not up to
     date, the number of history identities, the number of people listed,
     where the commit is pushed to, and the changes to \cb{AUTHORS}.|

     \li|Ask for confirmation, unless \cb{--yes} is specified.|

     \li|Install each \cb{AUTHORS} with a single rename, then commit and, if
     requested, push it.||

     For example, to update and commit the \cb{AUTHORS} of the repository in
     the current working directory:

     \
     $ authors identities

     repository:         /src/hello
     history identities: 5
     people listed:      2

     --- a/AUTHORS
     +++ b/AUTHORS
     @@ -7,3 +7,4 @@
      below once, by their preferred name, in alphabetical order:

      Jane Doe
     +John Roe <john@example.org>

     update '/src/hello/AUTHORS'? [y/n]: y
     \

     The commit subject is \cb{Update AUTHORS} and its message body lists the
     entries the update adds and removes, for example:

     \
     Update AUTHORS

       add    John Roe <john@example.org>
     \

     \h#entries|ENTRIES|

     The identity register is the one \l{mailmap(1)} reads (see its IDENTITY
     REGISTER section for the format and the rules). Its \cb{authors} value
     specifies how \cb{AUTHORS} lists the person:

     \dl|

     \li|\cb{name}

     By the preferred name. This is the default.|

     \li|\cb{email}

     By the preferred name and email.|

     \li|\cb{none}

     Not at all, for example, a bot.||

     For example:

     \
     : 1
     name: Jane Doe
     email: jane@example.org
     :
     name: John Roe
     email: john@example.org
     authors: email
     :
     name: Build Bot
     email: bot@example.org
     authors: none
     \

     Each history identity is attributed to its person the same way
     \l{mailmap(1)} maps it. Each entry \cb{AUTHORS} already lists is
     attributed the same way, by its name and email or, if it has no email,
     by a name the register records for exactly one person. The entry is then
     written the way the register currently lists its person, so a person
     leaves \cb{AUTHORS} only through \cb{authors: none}.

     Every history identity and every entry must be attributed. The command
     fails if the register does not attribute some of them, listing each
     one, for example:

     \
     error: history of '/src/hello' has identities not in 'identities'
       info: Stranger <stranger@example.net>
     \

     The people listed must also be told apart by their entries. Two people
     with the same name are refused unless the register lists them with
     \cb{authors: email}.
     "
  }

  class options
  {
    "\h|OPTIONS|"

    bool --all|-a
    {
      "Operate on every repository found under \i{dir}. Without this option,
       the repository \i{dir} is in is operated on. The search does not
       descend into a repository working tree, so nested repositories
       (submodules, repositories checked out inside another one) are never
       visited. Linked worktrees are skipped as well, since their repository
       is visited through its main working tree. Hidden directories are
       searched."
    };

    bool --no-commit
    {
      "Don't commit the updated \cb{AUTHORS}. By default, \cb{AUTHORS} is
       committed and only it is, whatever else is staged."
    };

    bool --push
    {
      "Push the commit to the upstream of the current branch. To push exactly
       this commit, the branch must not be ahead of its upstream before the
       update. If the push fails, then the commit is kept and the rest of the
       repositories are still updated."
    };

    bool --show-push
    {
      "Print the push command without executing it. This allows examining
       the commit before pushing it. Note that the command is printed to
       \cb{stdout}."
    };

    bool --print-only
    {
      "Print to \cb{stdout} what would be done without actually doing
       anything."
    };

    bool --yes|-y
    {
      "Don't prompt for confirmation before updating \cb{AUTHORS}."
    };

    bool --help
    {
      "Print usage information and exit."
    };

    bool --version
    {
      "Print version and exit."
    };
  };

  "
  \h|EXIT STATUS|

  Non-zero exit status is returned in case of an error, including when the
  update is not confirmed. Nothing is changed in any repository if the error
  is diagnosed before the confirmation.
  "
}
